```

Wait, that's the same shape. The compiler says the temporary (JavaStr) is created and the borrow might be used when the temporary is dropped at the end of the block — because `?` on `env.get_string(&jstr)?` creates a temporary that gets dropped at the end of the enclosing block (the if-else arm), but `to_string_lossy()` borrows from it. Saving into a local first should fix it since the temp is dropped at the end of the `let` statement... Actually the compiler's suggested fix is exactly:

```rust
