};
```

The difference from before: `text` is a named local, so the JavaStr temporary is dropped at the end of the `let text = ...` statement, before `Some(text)` is built. That should satisfy the borrow checker... Actually wait — with `let text = expr;`, the temporary JavaStr is dropped at the end of the full let statement. Then `Some(text)` uses the owned String. That should work.

Hmm, but actually the error says "consider forcing this temporary to be dropped sooner" — the suggested fix `let x = ...; x` — same idea. Let me apply.

